This image is an enlarged crop of a scanned # San Francisco zoning map. It is # pixels wide and # pixels high (origin top-left, x to the right, y down).

Heavy black lines are zoning district borders; thin lines are block and lot lines. Each district has its zoning code printed in it in large slanted hand lettering: for example RH-#, RH-#(D), RH-#, RH-#, RM-#, RM-#, RM-#, RC-#, NC-#, NC-#, NC-#, C-#, C-M, M-#, M-#, P, or a name such as "VALENCIA NCD". A code can run vertically or slanted, be crossed by lines, or lie over a block number. When a district is too small to hold its code, the code is printed outside it, in a street or a neighbouring block, often with a thin leader line or arrow running from the code into the district.
Small #- or #-digit numbers are block numbers and small capitals are street names: they are not zoning codes.

One area is tinted yellow. Heavy lines close it all round: it is one zoning district. Which zoning code does the map give the tinted area? List each code that is meant for it:
- printed inside the tinted area, whichever way it reads;
- or printed outside it with a leader line or arrow that runs from the code into the tinted area;
- or printed outside it, right beside it, with no heavy line between the code and the tinted area (or overlapping the heavy line around it).

For each one give:
- text: the code exactly as printed;
- x#, y#, x#, y#: the tightest box around its letters, in pixels of this image;
- direction: the way the text reads;
- where: one of the three cases above;
- legible: clear, partly hidden, or a guess.

Do not report a code that belongs to a neighbouring district (it sits inside that district, across a heavy line, with no leader into the tinted area). Do not invent a code: if the map prints no zoning code for the tinted area, return an empty list.

This image is a crop of a scanned # San Francisco zoning map. It is # pixels wide and # pixels high (origin top-left, x to the right, y down).

List EVERY piece of printed text in it, including text that is partly cut off at the image edge:
- zoning codes (e.g. RH-#, RH-#, RM-#, NC-#, NC-#, P, C-M, "VALENCIA NCD"), written large inside districts, sometimes vertically or touching lines;
- block numbers (#-digit numbers like #, small, inside blocks);
- street names and their "ST." / "AVE." (small capitals along streets, sometimes vertical);
- anything else (kind "other").

For each one give:
- text: exactly as printed, letter for letter. One item per whole label: all the words printed together as one name on one line are ONE item with one box, e.g. "#TH-NOE VALLEY NCD", "VALENCIA NCD", "CASTRO NCD", "TWENTY-FIRST ST." when ST. follows the name on the same line. A street's "ST." printed elsewhere, away from its name, is its own item;
- kind;
- x#, y#, x#, y#: the tightest box around the printed letters themselves, in pixels of this image; not around nearby lines;
- direction: the way the text reads;
- angle_deg: angle of the text's baseline counterclockwise from horizontal (# = normal, # = reads bottom to top, -# = reads top to bottom).

Do not invent text. Do not include lines, arrows or leader lines.

